{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791440850,"nonce":"1nUDQGGOjhzq0LHJObEh2cZBdNM=","title":"Generic function calls pass Float slots through the integer calling convention","message":"Native Haxe prints 3 and CONFORMANCE_OK for /tmp/GenericAbstractFunctionCall.hx (/tmp/rayzor-generic-abstract-function-native-v1.log). The pinned Cranelift path prints 0 and throws \"generic abstract function argument ABI\" (/tmp/rayzor-generic-abstract-function-mir-v1.log).\n\nThe reproducer uses an abstract CallableValue\u003cT\u003e(T-\u003eT) with only a transparent from T-\u003eT clause, and an inline apply(value:T):T method that calls this(value). It contains no @:from method or unsafe cast. The Float caller correctly stores 1.5 as i64 bits in the generic argument slot. The method calls the closure using that erased slot, but the concrete closure expects a native Float argument. The result is also returned across an erased slot.\n\nThe runtime already registers a boxed argument/result entry for each closure. Generic function calls should use a representation that agrees with the concrete closure ABI, including unboxing the returned value back into the caller's erased or concrete slot. This remains separate from matching generic function @:from methods (c10f3ebc0f0c8b2e6cee3d545a1a918ee3b8684bf80375fa8d2fd4f9dc320108).","files":null}]}